Du har ikke lagt ved detaljene for de tre abonnementene, så her er en representativ sammenligning av tre typiske nivåer (liten / mellom / stor pakke) i det norske markedet:

:::intelligent-ui{v="1"}
root = Comparison("Mobilabonnement – tre typiske nivåer", [p1, p2, p3])
p1 = {name:"Basis", attributes:["3 GB data","199 kr/mnd","4G-nett","Ingen bindingstid","EU-roaming: nei"]}
p2 = {name:"Pluss", attributes:["15 GB data","349 kr/mnd","5G-nett","Ingen bindingstid","EU-roaming: inkludert"]}
p3 = {name:"Fri", attributes:["Ubegrenset data","529 kr/mnd","5G-nett","Ingen bindingstid","EU-roaming: ubegrenset"]}
:::

**Anbefaling:** Pluss er som regel det beste kompromisset – 15 GB dekker de fleste, og du får 5G og EU-roaming uten å betale for ubegrenset data du sjelden bruker. Velg Basis hvis du er mest på Wi-Fi, og Fri hvis du streamer eller bruker mobilen som hotspot mye.

Tallene over er eksempelverdier. Send meg de faktiske navnene, prisene og datamengdene på de tre abonnementene du vil sammenligne, så setter jeg opp en presis sammenstilling – gjerne i en tabell med alle vilkår (pris, data, roaming, bindingstid, oppsigelsestid).

Here's a side-by-side look at the two packages:

:::intelligent-ui{v="1"}
root = Stack([cmp1, t1])
cmp1 = Comparison("Starter vs Pro", [p1, p2])
p1 = {name:"Starter", attributes:["1 project","5 GB storage","Community support","Core features","Monthly billing"]}
p2 = {name:"Pro", attributes:["Unlimited projects","100 GB storage","Priority support","Core + advanced features","Monthly or annual billing"]}
t1 = Table("Feature detail", ["Feature","Starter","Pro"], [["Projects","1","Unlimited"],["Storage","5 GB","100 GB"],["Support","Community","Priority"],["Advanced features","—","Included"],["Billing","Monthly","Monthly / annual"]])
:::

The Starter package is aimed at trying things out or running a single small project, while Pro removes the project and storage limits and adds priority support plus the advanced feature set. Pro becomes the better value once you need more than one project or faster support response.

Here is the typical onboarding sequence, with the current stage marked:

:::intelligent-ui{v="1"}
root = Stack([tl1, d1, m1])
tl1 = Timeline("Onboarding events, in order", [e1, e2, e3, e4, e5, e6, e7])
e1 = {title:"Account created", timestamp:"Day 0, 09:02", description:"Sign-up form submitted, workspace provisioned", status:"done"}
e2 = {title:"Email verified", timestamp:"Day 0, 09:04", description:"Confirmation link clicked", status:"done"}
e3 = {title:"Profile completed", timestamp:"Day 0, 09:11", description:"Name, role and time zone set", status:"done"}
e4 = {title:"Workspace connected", timestamp:"Day 0, 09:20", description:"Data source authorized", status:"active"}
e5 = {title:"Team invited", timestamp:"Day 0", description:"Teammates invited by email", status:"pending"}
e6 = {title:"First project created", timestamp:"Day 1", description:"Template chosen and shared", status:"pending"}
e7 = {title:"Guided tour finished", timestamp:"Day 1", description:"Onboarding marked complete", status:"pending"}
d1 = Divider()
m1 = Metric("Steps completed", "3 of 7", "Current stage: Workspace connected", "warning")
:::

The first three steps happen back-to-back in the first minutes and need nothing from the user beyond email confirmation. The real drop-off risk sits at **Workspace connected**, where an external authorization is required — everything after it (inviting the team, creating the first project, the tour) only starts once that succeeds.

